WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.830
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hi, I'm Priyanka.

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:04.410
And today, I'll be talking
to you about serverless API

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:05.310
development.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.200
After a quick
introduction, I'll also

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.600
provide a demo of how
to build and manage

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.540
serverless APIs with the
Google Cloud API Gateway.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.871
So stick around.

00:00:13.871 --> 00:00:16.817
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.980
Adoption of serverless computing
offers a profound paradigm

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.400
shift in developers' ability
to create highly scalable

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.470
applications and services.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.050
This architecture
considerably improves the way

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.660
modern applications and
services are created

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.360
by allowing developers
to build without worrying

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.730
about the setup and
maintenance of the underlying

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:42.930
infrastructure.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.130
And by adding an API facade to
these serverless applications,

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.800
developers can
seamlessly connect data,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.970
integrate systems, and
build modern applications.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.450
As organizations are
expanding their API programs,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.970
a key question
continues to come up--

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.540
how do we make APIs
lightweight on the client side

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.140
yet highly scalable and secure
to meet the changing traffic

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.430
demands?

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.860
To accomplish this,
many organizations

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.500
have been migrating their
business-critical APIs

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.360
to serverless architecture.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.610
Developers package
services functions

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.720
as RESTful APIs to increase
security of their services

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.540
with monitoring and
controlling access to data,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.490
protecting these
services from bot

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.920
attacks and other
malicious traffic,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:32.220
improving performance with
rate limiting and caching,

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.880
and identifying important
insights from analytics data

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:40.350
extracted from monitoring,
alerting, logging, and tracing.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.020
With Google Cloud
API Gateway, you

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.960
can create, secure,
and monitor APIs

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.630
for Google Cloud
serverless back ends.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:52.260
It enables you to provide
secure access to your back end

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:54.960
services through a
well-defined REST

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.650
API that is consistent
across all your services

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.570
regardless of the
service implementation.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:04.530
It can manage APIs for
multiple back ends,

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.729
including Cloud Functions,
Cloud Run, App Engine, Compute

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:11.700
Engine, and Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:16.590
Built on Envoy, API Gateway is
fully managed by Google Cloud

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.040
to give you high performance,
scalability, and the freedom

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.230
to focus on development.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.530
API Gateway gives you all
the operational benefits

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.960
of serverless APIs
while reducing

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.100
the complexity of
implementation,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.070
such as flexible
deployment and scalability,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:37.020
so that you can focus on
building great applications.

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:39.480
Additionally, Google
Cloud API Gateway

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.020
adds layers of security
control, such as authentication

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.480
and key validation,
by configuring

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.290
security definitions that
require all incoming calls

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.780
to provide a valid API key.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.140
It also allows you to set
quotas and specific usage

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.680
limits to protect your APIs
from abuse or excessive usage.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.230
Let's take a quick look.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.720
Here at Google,
we continue to see

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.720
a lot of developers
building their APIs on top

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.040
of microservices.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.370
So in this example, we've
deployed a weather API

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.780
on Cloud Run.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.690
It's made up of four
different services.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.360
This is great for you
as an API provider,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.850
because it gives you
a lot of flexibility

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.060
around how you deploy and
scheme these services.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.340
But as an API
consumer, you don't

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.470
want to keep track
of which service

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:31.440
is responsible for
which piece of the API.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.750
You just want one
interface that you

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.870
can call to access all
the capabilities provided

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.560
by this API.

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.480
So let's take a look at how
Google Cloud's API Gateway can

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.630
help you combine all
these services into one

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:47.340
easy-to-use API.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.790
To get started, we will need
an OpenAPI specification

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.220
that defines each of the
resources in our API.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.220
Next, we will add
instructions that

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.790
tell the API gateway where
to route the request for each

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.080
of those resources.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.050
And finally, we will
want to secure our APIs.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.930
So we will configure
a security definition

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:11.370
that requires all the incoming
calls to provide a valid API.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:14.310
Once we have our
OpenAPI specification

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.100
and we are ready to
create our API gateway,

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.470
first we'll specify
the display name

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.740
and give our API a
unique identifier.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.400
Next, we will define the
API's initial configuration

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.520
by uploading our OpenAPI file.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.730
We'll then give our
configuration a display name

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.620
and configure the
service accounts

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.720
that will be used to call
our back end services.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.370
And finally, we are ready to
deploy our API configuration

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.270
to a new gateway.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.100
In this case, that gateway will
run in the US central region.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.870
Once we hit Create,
after a few minutes

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:53.400
our gateway will be ready to
start accepting incoming API

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.120
requests.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.140
Because we have secured our API,
we will need valid credentials.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:02.250
In this case, we have elected
to use API keys as our security

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:03.090
mechanism.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:07.440
So we can get an API key for
our API in the APIs and Service

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.960
section of the
Google Cloud console.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.660
Once we have our key,
we can make an API call

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.940
from the command
line using Chrome.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:18.690
If we tried to access our
API without providing a key,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.140
the call would be
affected by the gateway.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.000
Once we add our
key to the request,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.170
the gateway then allows our
call to go through successfully.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.450
That was just a
quick preview of how

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.140
you can use Google Cloud API
Gateway to create and secure

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.220
your APIs.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:37.490
You can leverage API Gateway
for a number of important use

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:41.240
cases, such as abstracting back
end complexity to facilitate

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.150
development for web and
mobile applications;

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:48.630
connecting users to in-house and
third-party APIs and services,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:52.160
especially those provided
by Google Cloud; controlling

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.430
access for partners while rate
limiting the number of calls

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:59.360
from each partner; and extending
data services to enable

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.540
frictionless service-oriented
access to data.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:04.940
Now it's your turn
to try it out.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:07.970
Get started by clicking the
link in the description below.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:11.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

